To find the inverse of $f(x)$, we set $y = f(x)$ and solve for $x$.
\begin{align*}
y &= \frac{16}{5 + 3x} \\
\Rightarrow \quad 5 + 3x &= \frac{16}{y} \\
\Rightarrow \quad 3x &= \frac{16}{y} - 5 \\
\Rightarrow \quad x &= \frac{\frac{16}{y} - 5}{3} \\
\Rightarrow \quad f^{-1}(y) &= \frac{\frac{16}{y} - 5}{3}
\end{align*}
To find $f^{-1}(2)$, we substitute $y = 2$ into the equation for $f^{-1}(y)$:
\begin{align*}
f^{-1}(2) &= \frac{\frac{16}{2} - 5}{3} \\
&= \frac{8 - 5}{3} \\
&= \frac{3}{3} \\
&= 1
\end{align*}
Finally, we find $\left[f^{-1}(2)\right]^{-2}$:
\begin{align*}
\left[f^{-1}(2)\right]^{-2} &= 1^{-2} \\
&= 1
\end{align*}
The value of $\left[f^{-1}(2)\right]^{-2}$ is $\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1

