To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of $50$ and $5005$, we can use the Euclidean algorithm.
\begin{align*}
5005 &= 100 \cdot 50 + 5 \\
50 &= 10 \cdot 5 + 0
\end{align*}
Since the remainder is $0$, the GCD of $50$ and $5005$ is $5$.

To find the least common multiple (LCM) of $50$ and $5005$, we can use the prime factorization method.
\begin{align*}
50 &= 2 \cdot 5^2 \\
5005 &= 5 \cdot 7 \cdot 11 \cdot 13
\end{align*}
The LCM is the product of the highest powers of all prime factors. In this case, the LCM is $2 \cdot 5^2 \cdot 7 \cdot 11 \cdot 13 = 2 \cdot 5 \cdot 5005 = 50050$.

The sum of the GCD and LCM is $5 + 50050 = \boxed{50055}$.The answer is: 50055
